<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam Botanical Garden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1735</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Pamplemousses Botanical Garden, Pamplemousses Garden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seewoosagur-ramgoolam">Seewoosagur Ramgoolam</a> Botanical Garden, long known as the Pamplemousses Garden, is a botanical garden in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and one of the oldest in the southern hemisphere. Begun in 1735 as the private garden of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-de-la-bourdonnais">Mahé de La Bourdonnais</a>, it was developed from 1767 by the intendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre">Pierre Poivre</a>, who planted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> to break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> monopoly. It is famous for its giant water lilies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sir-seewoosagur-ramgoolam-botanical-garden.jpg" alt="Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam Botanical Garden" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Condem at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_water_lilies.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
